quest we  had to make was that as many of our  party as possible be accommodated near  the chief envoy's camp because of our  number and the amount of our baggage the  heads of the mission were evidently  anxious because if different members of  the party were scattered in remote  hotels it might be inconvenient and  unsafe in a strange land the host of the  welcoming committee understood nodded  his approval and asked the number of our  party when he was told it was 40 he  replied what if you're only 40 why one  of our hotels could accommodate ten or  twenty times that number we did not  comprehend what he meant but on reaching  the hotel assigned to us we found that  he was not jesting our headquarters were  the hotel de louvre opposite the  entrance to the Imperial Palace it  really was a huge edifice of five  storeys with 600 rooms and over 500  employees more than a thousand guests  could be accommodated at one time  instead of our anxiety unless the party  might be separated in distant hostelries  our real anxiety became the possibility  of losing our way in the maze of halls  and corridors in the one hotel numerous  gas lights served to illuminate the  rooms and halls so that we could not  distinguish at all the coming of  darkness outside in the dining hall  there was such a spread of food of both  the woods and the sea but even those in  the mission who professed their dislike  of foreign objects could not maintain  this aversion in the choice of food the  joke was in the stock of Japanese  supplies brought along in our baggage we  could not cook our rice in the kitchen  of the hotel nor was it possible within  reason to use our oil wick lamps in the  horse  finally exhausted with all the useless  impediments we piled it all up in an  apartment and offered the entire stores  rice oil lamps and everything to one of  the lesser members of the welcoming  committee and asked him to take it for  free as we were unfamiliar with Western  life and customs there was naturally no  end of the farcical situations occurring  among our party when one of the Lord  envis had occasion to use the toilet he  was followed to the doorway by one of  his personal attendants who carried the  lighted paper lantern as is the custom  in our homeland the attendant in his  most formal dress was to be seen  squatting patiently outside the open  door holding his master's removed sword  this happened to be in the bustling  corridor of the hotel where people were  passing constantly and the gas was  burning as bright as day  but unperturbed sat the faithful  guardian I happened to come along and  see the incident which I ended by  shutting the doors and turning to the  man I told him quietly of the etiquette  of the Europeans on such occasions but  my heart was fluttering with  consternation of the political situation  of that time I tried to learn as much as  I could from various persons that I met  in London and Paris though it was often  difficult to understand things clearly  as I was as yet so unfamiliar with the  history of Europe however I find now as  I look in my diary the mention of  Napoleon the third is a very powerful  figure said to be the greatest statesman  of all Europe at that time then I find  mention of a great growing power Prussia  the neighbouring country with our  influence spreading like a Rising Sun  the wars with Austria and the problem of  alsace-lorraine were constantly  discussed and I remember they were made  the subject of prophecy by many men  while we were in London certain member  of the Parliament sent us a copy of a  bill which he said he had proposed in  the house under the name of the party  which he belonged the bill was a protest  against the arrogant attitude of the  British Minister to Japan Allcock who  had at times acted as if Japan were a  country conquered by military force one  of the instances mentioned in the bill  was out of all [ __ ] riding his horse  into the sacred temple grounds of Sheba  an unpardonable insult to the Japanese  on reading the copy of this bill I felt  as if a load had been lifted from my  chest  after all the foreigners were not all  Devils  I felt that Japan was enduring some  pointed affronts on the part of the  foreign ministers who presumed on the  ignorance of our government but now that  I had actually come to the minister's  native land I found that there were  among them some truly impartial and  warm-hearted human beings so after this  I grew even more determined in my  doctrine of free communication with the  rest of the world  a perplexing institution was  representative government when they  asked a gentleman what the election law  was and what kind of an institution the  Parliament really was he simply replied  with a smile meaning I suppose that no  intelligent person was expected to ask  such a question but these were the  things most difficult of all for me to  understand in this connection I learned  that there were different political  parties the liberal and the conservative  who were always fighting against each  other in the government for some time it  was beyond my comprehension to  understand what they were fighting for  and what was meant anyway by fighting in  peacetime this man and that man are  enemies in the house they would tell me  but these enemies were to be seen at the  same table eating and drinking with each  other I felt as if I could not take much  out of this it took me a long time with  some tedious thinking before I could  gather a general notion of these  separate mysterious facts some of the  more complicated matters I might achieve  an understanding five or ten days after  they were explained to me but all in all  I learned much from this initial tour of  Europe the country in Europe which gave  us the kindest welcome was Holland  which was a natural outcome of the very  special relationship which Japan had  enjoyed with Holland for the last 300  years moreover all the members of the  mission who knew any foreign language at  all had studied Dutch before any other  language so it made Holland so far as  the use of language was concerned seemed  like our second homeland there was I  recall an episode which was rather  significant one day in Amsterdam during  a conversation with some merchants and  other gentlemen our envoi chance to ask  the question is the sale and purchase of  land in Amsterdam freely permitted the  reply was certainly it is free do you  sell land to foreigners also yes as long  as a foreigner who's willing to pay for  that land we would sell any amount of it  to any person then suppose a foreigner  were to put down a large sum of money to  purchase a great tract of land in order  to build a fortress would you allow that  to the Hollanders looked puzzled at this  and replied we never have had occasion  to think of such a case even though  there are many rich men in England and  France and other lands we do not believe  any merchant would spend money on such a  venture  neither side understood the other we  interpreters were much amused by this  conversation it is not to be wondered at  that Japan was going through a hard  struggle when the control and handling  of foreign affairs were in the hands of  men who exhibited such reasoning in  their contact with the West one  ridiculous idea held by our embassy was  that its members should not meet the  foreigners or see the country any more  than they had to we were under the  seclusion theory even while we were  traveling in foreign territory  here by the way I I have to admit a  weakness of mine  I've always been lively and rather  inclined to being boastful but the truth  is I am of a timid nature when it comes  to putting a living thing to death or  seeing any kind of injury so while we  were in Russia this faint-hearted human  was taken to see a major surgical  operation my friends  Mizuki and Matt Sookie were eager to go  because they were both physicians by  profession they induced me to go along  in the operating room of the hospital  the surgeon in a kind of raincoat was  giving the patient chloroform on a table  which looked to me like a carving board  then he took her shining knife and  pierced the side of the patient Wow a  tremendous burst of blood shot out on  the raincoat of the surgeon the  operation was for a stone I kept on  looking at his movements until the  inserted an instrument like a pair of  pliers in the wound to lift out the  stone then I began to grow faint one of  my companions helped me out of the room  and gave me water ever since we had  started on the tour the auntie foreign  movement in Japan had been growing worse  and more and more blunders were being  made in our foreign diplomacy in Russia  our envoy's attempted to settle the  dispute over the border line I wasn't  present at the actual discussion of the  dispute so I know how they ridiculed our  protests our invoice thoughtfully spread  out their map and pointed out that  according to the diagram in which  Japanese territory was printed blue and  Russian territory who read the border  line would be such and such therefore  Japan would naturally claim all the land  within that line the Russians thereupon  retorted that if the colors on the map  were to decide the extent of national  territory they could easily claim the  whole world by painting the entire map  read this Pleasant raillery made it  impossible for the Japanese envoy's to  conclude any argument finally they were  obliged to resign their efforts  I felt thoroughly discouraged after  sitting through this negotiation  what hope for the future of Japan as  long as our people showed this  stiff-necked pride keeping aloof from  the actual give-and-take of the rest of  the civilized world the more this  movement of expel the foreigners  increased the more we would lose our  national power to say nothing of  prestige I was mortified when I thought  over the possible outcomes of national  exclusiveness one day it 